Smallest Range Covering Elements from K Sorted Lists

⚠️ This problem is NOT PRESENT anywhere in your repository list.

🧠 Problem Statement

You are given K sorted lists of integers.

Your task is to find the smallest range [L, R] such that each of the K lists contributes at least one number within this range.

📘 Example
Input
nums = [
  [4, 10, 15, 24, 26],
  [0, 9, 12, 20],
  [5, 18, 22, 30]
]

Output
[20, 24]

Explanation

List 1 → 24

List 2 → 20

List 3 → 22

All fall inside [20, 24], and this is the smallest possible range.

💡 Key Insight

This is like:

Merge K sorted lists, but always track:

Current minimum

Current maximum

We use:

Min Heap → to get smallest element

Track max value manually

🧩 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Insert first element of each list into a min heap

Track the current maximum

Pop the minimum element

Update range if smaller

Insert the next element from the same list

Stop when any list runs out

⏱ Time & Space Complexity
Metric	Value
Time	O(N log K)
Space	O(K)

N → total elements
K → number of lists

🧑‍💻 Java Code (Interview-Ready)
import java.util.*;

public class SmallestRangeKLists {

    static class Node {
        int value;
        int row;
        int col;

        Node(int value, int row, int col) {
            this.value = value;
            this.row = row;
            this.col = col;
        }
    }

    public static int[] smallestRange(List<List<Integer>> nums) {

        PriorityQueue<Node> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> a.value - b.value
        );

        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        // Step 1: Insert first element of each list
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            int val = nums.get(i).get(0);
            minHeap.add(new Node(val, i, 0));
            max = Math.max(max, val);
        }

        int rangeStart = 0, rangeEnd = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        // Step 2: Process heap
        while (true) {
            Node curr = minHeap.poll();

            int min = curr.value;

            // Step 3: Update best range
            if (max - min < rangeEnd - rangeStart) {
                rangeStart = min;
                rangeEnd = max;
            }

            // Step 4: Move to next element in same list
            if (curr.col + 1 == nums.get(curr.row).size()) {
                break; // one list exhausted
            }

            int nextVal = nums.get(curr.row).get(curr.col + 1);
            minHeap.add(new Node(nextVal, curr.row, curr.col + 1));
            max = Math.max(max, nextVal);
        }

        return new int[]{rangeStart, rangeEnd};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<Integer>> nums = new ArrayList<>();

        nums.add(Arrays.asList(4, 10, 15, 24, 26));
        nums.add(Arrays.asList(0, 9, 12, 20));
        nums.add(Arrays.asList(5, 18, 22, 30));

        int[] result = smallestRange(nums);
        System.out.println(result[0] + " " + result[1]);
    }
}

🔍 Line-by-Line Explanation
Node Class
class Node {
    int value, row, col;
}


➡ Stores:

value → current number

row → which list

col → index inside the list

Min Heap
PriorityQueue<Node> minHeap


➡ Always gives the smallest current element

Tracking Maximum
int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;


➡ Required to compute range [min, max]

Range Update Logic
if (max - min < rangeEnd - rangeStart)


➡ Always keep the smallest interval

Termination Condition
if (curr.col + 1 == nums.get(curr.row).size())
    break;


➡ If any list finishes → no valid K-covering range possible

